1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,098
A real, model-visible browser window, driven from inside a conversation.

2
00:00:04,098 --> 00:00:07,000
Let's turn it on and watch it open and read a page.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,138
Turn on the Browser tool group.

4
00:00:09,138 --> 00:00:17,000
Once it's on, ask the agent to open a page and summarize something on it — that's the whole loop for this episode.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:25,988
The tool list: open_url loads a page, read_all_text and find_text pull content off it, list_buttons and click_button let it interact, and take_screenshot or read_screenshot let it look.

6
00:00:25,988 --> 00:00:29,000
That's enough to browse, search, and click through most pages.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,633
Why a real positioned window, not an iframe embedded in the app?

8
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:35,747
Cross-origin iframes can't be scripted from outside, and most real sites refuse to be framed at all.

9
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:42,000
So the app opens an actual browser window and positions it — which is also why that panel holds still below the chat instead of scrolling along with it.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:53,000
One view, no tabs — this browser follows one page at a time, the way you'd expect from something the model is reading directly rather than juggling.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:58,978
The gotcha: the in-app browser cannot take a screenshot — that capability needs the browser extension, which is next episode.

12
00:00:58,978 --> 00:01:04,000
Combined with having only one view and no tabs, it's built for reading and clicking, not for visual work.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
For a session you're already signed into, or for real screenshots and tabs, the browser extension is the next step.

